I am designing an algorithm to invert a given neural net. I want a wrapper class which takes as input a feedforward neural net. The feed forward net is assumed to have a field "layers" which is an array of the layers (functions) to be called in order. At instantiation, I run a dummy input through the feedforward net and store the intermediate shapes of activations between layers. Then, instantiate additional weights with the shape of the activations between layers corresponding space.

These intermediate weights are "offsets" to be applied between layers by adding them to the activations of the preceding layer. When inverting my network, I optimize over both these offsets and the input to the neural net to match a given output as best as possible. 

The loss concists of the mistmatch of the output of the neural net (an MSE) to the true image. There is also an l2 regularisation on all offset vectors. During the recovery process, I want to gradually increase the strength of the l2 reg. on the offsets (only the offsets, not the first input to the network). The increase should be exponential, starting at a very low value, and increasing by factors of 2 until the constraint is very strong. Between each increase, use LBFGS to optimize with three steps. After a sufficient number of increases, the offsets should be dropped completely. At all stages, every loss term is optimized jointly. There is no l2 regularization on the input to the neural net itself, and the strength of the final MSE remains unchaged at all stages.

